# Phase 3 — Evaluation Render Harness

Renders each mesh (high-poly reference + every `ratio_X` decimated mesh) from a
fixed, identical camera + lighting rig, so that Phase 4 can compute image-level
metrics (PSNR/SSIM) that reflect actual geometry/texture differences — not
differences caused by inconsistent framing or lighting.

## Directory layout

```
pipeline/phase3_evaluate/
    render_harness.py      # this script (Blender headless)
    README.md              # this file
```

Input (already produced by Phase 1 / Phase 2):

```
data/outputs/<run_id>/phase1_highpoly/mesh.obj      (+ texture.png)
data/outputs/<run_id>/phase2_lowpoly/ratio_0.5/mesh.obj (+ texture.png)
data/outputs/<run_id>/phase2_lowpoly/ratio_0.2/mesh.obj
data/outputs/<run_id>/phase2_lowpoly/ratio_0.1/mesh.obj
data/outputs/<run_id>/phase2_lowpoly/ratio_0.05/mesh.obj
```

Output:

```
data/outputs/<run_id>/phase3_renders/
    highpoly/view_00.png ... view_11.png
    ratio_0.5/view_00.png ... view_11.png
    ratio_0.2/...
    ratio_0.1/...
    ratio_0.05/...
```

Same view indices across every folder → Phase 4 pairs by filename.

## Usage

```powershell
blender --background --python .\pipeline\phase3_evaluate\render_harness.py -- --run-dir "data/outputs/chair_run1"
```

Optional flags: `--views` (informational only right now — see design decisions
below), `--resolution` (default 1024), `--ring-views` (default 8),
`--top-views` (default 4).

## Design decisions

- **12-view 2-ring camera rig**: 8 views in a lower ring at 15° elevation
  (catches ground-level silhouette errors), 4 views in an upper ring at 55°
  elevation offset in azimuth from the lower ring (catches top/pole geometry
  a flat turntable would miss).
- **Camera bbox/distance locked to the high-poly mesh, computed once**: every
  ratio_X mesh is rendered through the *exact same* camera positions. If each
  mesh's own bbox were used instead, shrinkage from aggressive decimation
  would get silently re-framed to fill the frame the same way every time —
  masking exactly the distortion we're trying to measure. This is the
  standard convention in mesh-simplification/neural-rendering evaluation
  literature: one fixed rig derived from a reference mesh, reused verbatim
  across all compared variants.
- **Renderer: Eevee (Blender 5.1 identifier `BLENDER_EEVEE`, internally
  "Eevee Next")** — chosen for headless batch speed on a 4GB VRAM card, at
  the cost of some physical accuracy vs Cycles. Revisit if Phase 4 results
  seem inconsistent with Chamfer distance and Cycles is worth the time cost.
- **Transparent background** (`film_transparent=True`, RGBA PNG output) — so
  PSNR/SSIM comparisons aren't influenced by background color/gradient
  choices.
- **Fixed 3-point lighting (key/fill/rim)**, sized relative to mesh radius
  and reused identically for every mesh in a run, so shading differences
  never confound geometry/texture differences. Energies were originally
  800/300/500W (blew out detail); reduced to 150/60/90W, routed through a
  single `LIGHT_BRIGHTNESS_MULTIPLIER` constant for easy one-line tuning.
- **Fixed FOV (50°)** on the camera, not derived per-mesh, for the same
  reason as the locked bbox above.

## Known issues encountered + fixes

| Issue | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `TypeError: enum "BLENDER_EEVEE_NEXT" not found` | Blender 5.1 kept the legacy `BLENDER_EEVEE` enum string even though the underlying engine is Eevee Next | Use `scene.render.engine = "BLENDER_EEVEE"` |
| Textures not appearing (flat grey/blank) | TripoSR outputs `mesh.obj` + `texture.png` as separate files with **no `.mtl`** linking them, so the OBJ importer has nothing to attach | After import, check each object for an existing image texture (`_has_image_texture`); if none, manually build a Principled BSDF material and wire `texture.png` into Base Color (`_attach_texture`) |
| Mesh imported sideways (needed manual −90° X rotation) | OBJ is Y-up by convention; Blender is Z-up; default importer axis args weren't matching | Pass `forward_axis="Y", up_axis="Z"` explicitly to `bpy.ops.wm.obj_import` |
| Renders looked blown-out / hard to spot errors | Light energies too high (800/300/500W) | Reduced to 150/60/90W + added `LIGHT_BRIGHTNESS_MULTIPLIER = 0.15` as a single tuning knob |
| Background looked black instead of transparent | Likely a viewer issue (Windows Photos renders transparent PNG regions as black instead of checkerboard), not a render bug — `film_transparent=True` + RGBA output is correct. Confirm by opening in a browser tab, GIMP, or Paint.NET instead. | No code change; verify with an alpha-aware viewer |

## Open / deferred items

- Camera distance/framing ("move camera closer to chair") — deferred until
  Phase 4 metrics are available. Only revisit if the numbers (not just visual
  impression) suggest framing is masking or exaggerating differences.
- Per-mesh-normalized evaluation (separate camera correction per ratio, as an
  addition to the fixed-rig approach) — explicitly out of scope for now,
  logged as a possible post-core-pipeline optimization if time permits.
- Texture re-baking strategy at aggressive decimation ratios — parked until
  evaluation results show whether it's actually needed.

## Dependencies (Phase 4 — install without touching the existing env)

Phase 3 itself only needs Blender's bundled Python (`bpy`), no extra
installs. Phase 4 (PSNR/SSIM/Chamfer/Hausdorff) needs a few new packages in
the `2d_to_3d` conda env. Because torchmcubes' CUDA build was fragile to set
up (pinned MSVC v143, CUDA 11.8, Ninja generator — see Phase 0 notes), **do
not let any new package silently upgrade or reinstall `torch`.**

Safe install sequence:

```powershell
conda activate 2d_to_3d

# Image metrics — lightweight, no torch dependency
pip install scikit-image

# Mesh distance metrics — trimesh is lighter and less likely to conflict
# than open3d (open3d pulls its own numpy/torch-adjacent deps and is a much
# heavier install; avoid it unless trimesh proves insufficient)
pip install trimesh scipy
```

After installing, **immediately verify torch/CUDA still work** before doing
anything else:

```powershell
python -c "import torch; print(torch.__version__, torch.cuda.is_available())"
```

If `torch.cuda.is_available()` prints `False` or the version changed
unexpectedly, something in the above pulled a conflicting dependency — stop
and roll back (`pip install torch==<original_version> --force-reinstall
--no-deps` using whatever version Phase 0 had) before continuing.

If `trimesh` turns out to be insufficient for a needed operation later
(e.g. more robust Hausdorff distance, mesh sampling utilities), evaluate
`open3d` as a fallback — but install it in a **separate venv** first to
confirm it doesn't conflict, rather than installing directly into
`2d_to_3d`.